  "text": "“Perhaps they don’t enjoy our stories anymore,” I thought. I could have gone to ask. Instead, I sat a little longer. Eventually I put the spare cushions away. The bench was easier to clear, but I did not like the look of it.\n\nThen their visits became less frequent. At first, I set out the cushions as usual. If I heard leaves scrape along the path, I looked up. Sometimes I had already begun to smile before I saw that nobody was there.\n\nBefore the storm, I put cushions on the garden bench every evening. I knew which corner of the bench stayed warm longest. I knew where to sit so I could see the path and still leave room for someone beside me.\n\nI had assumed my friends no longer cared about the garden. Yet Mara had written a story for me, and Sol had brought his page. I had been looking at the empty places beside me and guessing what they meant.\n\nA section can reach the bank and still be loose. If you step onto an unsecured section, it may slip. Keep the secured part in place and recover the loose materials. You can use the same materials again.\n\nThose two lanterns hold The Windy Picnic and The Unexpected Duet. I still like opening them, even though I know what happens. The waiting flowers have room for the stories Mara, Sol and I share next.\n\nLook for a crossing where both sections together can reach the banks. Place the first section against the near bank. Seat its two near corner posts and its two center posts in their marked sockets.\n\nThese are my two reusable footbridge sections. Six posts and two ropes are beside them. The posts support the ropes along the two sides of the walkway. Keep the middle clear for walking. —Grandma",
